<Layout title="How Long Until It Works? — Parenting" description="Ambika Agarwal, Dr. Deborah Tillman, Dr. Ashley Hofflander, Dr. Jazmine and more, summarized from their Instagram reels: how long parenting changes take to show results, why behavior gets worse before it gets better, and how to tell it's working.">
  <main class="bg-[#0c0b09] min-h-screen px-4 py-14 sm:py-20 text-[#ece5d3]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/parenting" class="text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline">← All parenting topics</a>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-[#c9a86a]">Topic 04 · Timelines</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight leading-[1.05] text-[#f7f0dc]">How long until it works?</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        Summarized from <strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">12 Instagram posts</strong> by Ambika Agarwal, Dr. Deborah Tillman,
        Dr. Ashley Hofflander, Ready Set Parent, Kayla (Behavior Babe), Taylor Olivia Hunt, Candice Lapin,
        Jane, Dr. Jazmine, Nicole Guy, Dr. Becky Kennedy, and Big Little Feelings. Every summary below links to the original.
      </p>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#c9a86a]/40 bg-[#c9a86a]/10 p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">The short answer</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xl leading-relaxed text-[#f7f0dc] font-medium">It takes longer than the internet says — and the first sign it's working is often that it gets worse. Change is a sequence, not a switch: a new response, a pushback, then a new default.</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The clock:</strong> the seed reel puts a calmer home at 2–4 weeks of no shouting and less self-doubt at 2–3 months of no comparisons — but the Ladder Method says lasting change takes roughly 18 months of practice, and Dr. Hofflander says strong-willed kids can escalate for two to three months. These are different clocks for different things.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The dip:</strong> when a behavior that used to work stops working, the child tries harder first — louder, longer — before it fades. That's an extinction burst, and it's the week most parents quit.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The test:</strong> don't judge by the immediate reaction. Real learning in children is often delayed — a child coming back days later, genuinely remorseful without being prompted, or trying again after failing.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The split:</strong> some experts say hold the line for 3–7 days and the worst tantrum means you're closest; others say a month of consistency with no improvement means look for other factors. Both agree on one thing: constantly switching methods is what makes results feel chaotic.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- The seed timelines -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The seed: one reel's timelines</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p>Ambika Agarwal's reel — the one that started this page — lists six habits with a timeline for each. These are her claims, not a promise: timelines vary by child and age, and the other experts on this page disagree on specifics, especially on whether two weeks is realistic.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">Quality time — 1–2 weeks.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: a stronger bond.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">No shouting — 2–4 weeks.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: a calmer home.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">Active listening — 2–6 weeks.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: more trust.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">Giving choices — 4–8 weeks.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: less resistance.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">No threats — 1–2 months.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: less lying and hiding.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">No comparisons — 2–3 months.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The payoff: less self-doubt.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">Her closing line, and the tone of the whole page: "Keep showing up, keep learning, keep trying. And remember you don't have to be a perfect parent to make a beautiful difference in your child's life — just keep growing."</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Why it gets worse -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Why it gets worse before it gets better</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. The vending machine — Dr. Hofflander.</strong> Every behavior is doing a job: it gets attention, or it gets your child out of something hard. Whatever you've been doing in response is the pattern they know, and it works like a vending machine button — A7 gets me my chips, and it always has. When you stop reinforcing it, the button stops working. Nobody presses a reliable machine once, gets nothing, and walks away. They press again, shake it, hit it harder. That escalation is your kid pressing harder. It isn't the plan failing.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. The sequence — Dr. Tillman.</strong> Change installs in a sequence: Problem → System → Resistance → Repeated Execution → Adaptation → New Default. The system is one predictable, unchanging response to a recurring behavior. The resistance — more crying, refusing, testing old behaviors — is a stage in the install. "The resistance is not the system failing. It is the last thing the old behavior tries."</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. The spike has numbers — Ready Set Parent.</strong> When a learned behavior stops being rewarded, the brain doesn't give up immediately; it tries harder first. They put numbers on it: the behavior spikes 50–70% before it drops, and the burst usually lasts 3–7 days. "The worst tantrum means you're closest to breakthrough" — but only if you hold the boundary through the spike. Cave on day 3 and you reset the clock — and teach the brain that bigger tantrums work.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. The burst has a long tail — Dr. Hofflander and Nicole Guy.</strong> Not every child bursts for a week. For some it takes a few firm rounds; for others two to three months, and strong-willed and neurodivergent kids can keep escalating for a while. Nicole Guy watches for the burst as the sign a change is coming — the child using their "last stops" — and says families often report no change as quickly as hoped, some regression, or slower-than-expected progress. That's normal. "Immediate, big progression is not always seen."</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">5. It can come back — Behavior Babe.</strong> There's a second pattern, distinct from the burst: spontaneous recovery. A behaviour that had faded returns weeks later — the child who stopped crying for candy at the store cries for it again. "It came back" doesn't mean the change failed; it's a known pattern, different from the burst, which happens immediately when reinforcement is first removed.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- The playbook -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The playbook: what to do while you wait</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88] leading-relaxed">Synthesized from all twelve sources. They disagree on the clock — see below — but they converge on the order of operations.</p>
        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">1 · Pick one system, not five.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Tillman: one predictable, unchanging response to one recurring behavior. Jane: get a plan, commit to it. A system you can actually repeat beats five half-started methods.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">2 · Expect the resistance — name it as a stage.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Tillman's sequence puts resistance in the middle, not at the end: it's what happens after the system starts, before the new default. Knowing it's a stage is what keeps you from reading it as failure.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">3 · Hold the line through the spike.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Ready Set Parent: name what's happening — "I know this is hard. The answer is still no." — don't negotiate, stay boring, stay calm. The meltdown isn't proof you're failing; it's proof the brain is unlearning an old pattern.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">4 · Build the replacement while you wait.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Hofflander: "In the meantime we're building the replacement, so there's somewhere else for them to go." Removing the old payoff without teaching a new way to get the need met leaves the child with nowhere to go.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">5 · Don't switch methods mid-burst.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Jane: "If you're constantly switching methods, reacting emotionally, or quitting when it gets hard, the results will always feel chaotic." Every switch restarts the clock the child is trying to read.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">6 · Measure by weeks, not days.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Hunt: behavior analysis is a toolbox — different jobs need different tools, some jobs take longer, and sometimes you need more tools for one job. You can't fix a leak with tape when you need to patch the hole. The Ladder Method: if change feels slow, "that doesn't mean it isn't working. It usually means it's finally becoming sustainable."</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">7 · Repair when you lose it.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Becky and Big Little Feelings, below: the timeline includes your own slip-ups. What matters isn't never messing up — it's what you do next, and repair is part of the change, not a reset of it.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- How to tell it's working -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">How to tell it's working</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. Don't judge by the immediate reaction — Dr. Jazmine.</strong> You give a consequence and the child doesn't cry, doesn't apologise, just walks away — so you wonder if anything landed. Many of us were taught to judge discipline by immediate visible results. Real learning in children is often delayed: it looks like the child coming back days later, genuinely remorseful without being prompted to say sorry, or trying again after failing without noticing. "Play the long game. I want us to start looking at parenting as character building which it takes years to develop. It's not going to happen with one perfect consequence."</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. Watch the return to baseline — Nicole Guy.</strong> After the burst, the child typically returns to baseline or shows improved behavior. Progress may look like regression first, and slower-than-expected progress is normal — the burst itself, the child using their "last stops," is the sign a change is coming.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. Track the stages, not the day — Dr. Tillman.</strong> The sequence gives you a map: resistance, then repeated execution, then adaptation — when the old behavior no longer changes the outcome — then a new default. If you can name where you are in the install, you don't have to guess whether anything is happening.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. The one-month check — Taylor Hunt.</strong> If you've been consistent for a month without improvement, that is the point to look for other factors — sleep, hunger, routine changes, environment — and to consider asking someone with different tools for help. A month of consistency with no movement is information, not failure.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Repair -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The other half of the timeline: repair</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Becky: it's what you tell yourself after.</strong> "What matters most isn't never messing up — it's what we do next." Her mantras: "Perfect parents don't exist. I'm a human, not a robot." "One moment doesn't define me. I am more than my latest behavior." "It's not the yelling that unravels us. It's how we talk to ourselves after." "I'm a good parent having a hard time. I don't lose my goodness when I lose my cool." "I can pause. I can breathe. I can repair. It's never too late." You don't have to be perfect to be a good parent; you just have to keep showing up.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Big Little Feelings: repair rewires the brain.</strong> "The way back matters more than the way apart." Yelling triggers a threat response — cortisol up, fight/flight/freeze, the thinking brain offline. Repair — grounding yourself, circling back, owning your reaction, helping them make sense of what happened — changes the encoding to safety, trust, and resilience. Their script: "I got frustrated. I yelled. That's not the best way to handle what I was feeling. I'm sorry. How did you feel? Sad, scared — yeah, that makes sense. It's not your fault — only I'm in charge of my reactions, we're all working on something, I'll keep working on it. I love you so much, you're a great kid." And the long game: repeated repairs wire the beliefs "I'm safe even when things get hard," "love doesn't disappear," and "conflict isn't something to fear" — the blueprint for how the child will love others.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Disagreements -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Where the experts disagree</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88] leading-relaxed">In their own words. No verdict here — the disagreement is the point.</p>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">How fast is fast?</strong> Agarwal: quality time in 1–2 weeks, no shouting in 2–4 weeks. The Ladder Method: "Lasting change can't really happen in a week" — transformation takes roughly 18 months of repetitive practice, and believing it should be quick "often sets people up to feel like they're failing." The two are different claims about different things — a calmer home in weeks, a locked-in skill in months — but a parent reading both gets two very different clocks, and no one on this page reconciles them.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">How long does the burst last?</strong> Ready Set Parent: the burst "usually lasts 3–7 days," and "the worst tantrum means you're closest to breakthrough." Hofflander: for some kids it takes a few firm rounds, for others two to three months, and strong-willed and neurodivergent kids "tend to keep escalating for a while." Nicole Guy: regression and slower-than-expected progress are normal. So the 3–7 day window is one account's number, not the field's — and the worst tantrum may be the middle, not the end.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Does the old behaviour coming back mean it failed?</strong> Behavior Babe: spontaneous recovery — weeks later the child may cry for candy again even though the behaviour had decreased — is a separate, normal pattern, not the burst and not failure. Ready Set Parent, talking about a different moment: "If you cave, you teach the brain that bigger tantrums work. The next boundary will require an even bigger meltdown." One is about a return after success; the other is about caving mid-burst. They don't contradict each other, but a parent has to know which one they're looking at.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">When do you stop waiting and change course?</strong> Hunt: if you've been consistent for a month without improvement, other underlying factors may be at play — sleep, hunger, routine changes, environment — and "we need to recognize moments when we should reach out for help/ask someone with diff tools/experience." Jane: the answer is to commit harder — "Get a plan. Commit to it. Play the long game" — and constantly switching methods is what makes results feel chaotic. One draws a line at a month; the other says the line is the problem.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">What counts as "working"?</strong> Dr. Jazmine: learning is delayed and quiet — a child coming back days later, genuinely remorseful without being prompted, or trying again after failing. Parenting is "character building which it takes years to develop." Tillman: the sequence has visible stages — resistance, then adaptation, then a new default — so you can tell exactly where you are in the install. One frames progress as invisible character-building over years; the other as a system with stages you can track.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Repair — whose brain?</strong> Becky: the damage is in the parent's self-talk after the yell — "It's not the yelling that unravels us. It's how we talk to ourselves after." Big Little Feelings: the damage and the repair are in the child's brain — yelling triggers a threat response; repair rewires it toward safety and trust. Both say repair matters; they locate the work differently.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- When to get support -->
      <section class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.04] p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">When it's more than a waiting problem</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">If you've been consistent for a month and nothing is moving — or the behaviour is escalating past the burst window, or your child seems to be struggling in ways that don't fit the pattern — the issue probably isn't patience, it's fit. Look at sleep, hunger, routine changes, and environment first (Hunt), and consider reaching out for help: a pediatrician, child psychologist, or parenting coach can sort out whether the behaviour needs a different skill, a different setup, or a different kind of support. The reels above are summaries, not a substitute for that conversation.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Sources -->
      <section class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The sources, summarized one by one</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88]">Everything above is drawn from these Instagram posts. Summaries are in our words; the originals are the experts'.</p>
        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5">
          {sources.map((s, i) => (
            <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
                <span class="text-[#c9a86a] font-bold">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">{s.title}</h3>
                <span class="text-xs rounded-full border border-white/15 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.format}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">{s.expert} · {s.handle}</p>
              <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.summary}</p>
              <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-3 inline-block text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline break-all">Watch on Instagram ↗ {s.url}</a>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#8a8474]">Compiled 2 November 2026.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">This page summarizes public social-media posts for personal reference. It is not medical or psychological advice.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
